  "track.sysdesign.i0.essence": "This track teaches system design seen from the Android app: what a developer's device does not show, such as process death, unreliable networks, multiple devices and old versions still running, following official docs and system design books.\nPart 1 (I1–I5) is the foundation: planning, processes and memory, threads and locks, choosing a channel, unreliable networks. Part 2 (I6–I12) covers the topics: caching, paging, conflicts, push, large files, startup, safe releases. I13 puts it all together in a messaging app.\nIf you are facing a specific bug, start from the symptom lookup table below.",
  "track.sysdesign.i0.goal.g1": "Saw when the symptom shows up",
  "track.sysdesign.i0.goal.g2": "Reached the chapter to read",
  "track.sysdesign.i0.sim.sysdesign.map": "symptoms and chapters",
  "track.sysdesign.i0.sec.hook.title": "Predict before reading",
  "track.sysdesign.i0.sec.hook.badge": "guideline",
  "track.sysdesign.i0.sec.hook.b.01.predict": "Of the 14 symptoms in the lookup table, how many do you guess a developer's device (office Wi-Fi, never killed, one device, always the latest version) would never hit?",
  "track.sysdesign.i0.sec.hook.b.01.ans": "Almost all of them. Eight only show in the real world, two need several or different devices, three only after a release, and one needs scale. This counts the site's table, not a statistic.",
  "track.sysdesign.i0.sec.order.title": "Reading order",
  "track.sysdesign.i0.sec.order.badge": "guideline",
  "track.sysdesign.i0.sec.order.b.01.p": "Read I1 first: it sets the habit of asking before designing. I2 and I3 are the foundation on processes and threads. I4 → I5 build on each other (choosing a channel, then handling its failures). I6 to I12 stand alone, though I8 uses I6's ETags, I9 uses I4 and I5, I10 uses I2 and I5. Read I13 last. Knowing Coroutines A1–A3 and Architecture G4 first helps.",
  "track.sysdesign.i0.sec.index.title": "Index: the visual of each chapter",
  "track.sysdesign.i0.sec.index.badge": "guideline",
  "track.sysdesign.i0.sec.index.b.01.p": "I1: a question table and an estimate table. I2: a process ladder and a RAM bar. I3: two-thread lanes and a wait-for graph. I4: a sequence diagram per channel. I5: a sequence diagram and retry lanes. I6: a three-tier cache table and a header table. I7: two strips, server and app. I8: three-party lanes and a per-field merge table. I9: a sequence diagram and a guarantees table. I10: an image memory worksheet and an upload chunk strip. I11: a startup timeline. I12: a day-by-day strip and a version matrix. I13: a component graph and a sequence diagram.",
  "track.sysdesign.i0.sec.links.title": "Bridges to other tracks",
  "track.sysdesign.i0.sec.links.badge": "guideline",
  "track.sysdesign.i0.sec.links.b.01.p": "I2 links to Compose Runtime (state management) through X17, I3 to Coroutines (shared state) through X18, I6 to Architecture G4 (source of truth) through X19. The outbox and WorkManager in I5, I10 and I13 use the mechanics of the Saving Data track H7.",
  "track.sysdesign.i0.sec.recall.title": "Answer it yourself",
  "track.sysdesign.i0.sec.recall.badge": "stable",
  "track.sysdesign.i0.sec.recall.b.01.ask": "Which four conditions does a developer's device usually lack when testing a design?",
  "track.sysdesign.i0.sec.recall.b.01.ans": "Weak or lost networks and a sleeping device; process death; several devices per user; old app versions still running when the server changes.",
